This would be a feature request for a narrower return type of `inject`, as the current signatures are not invalid. They just don't make a distinction between various injection flags.

Since `InjectFlags` is a bit mask, this requires special care. The following set of signature overloads makes this work as desired:

```ts
declare function inject<T>(token: Type<T>): T;
declare function inject<T>(token: Type<T>, flags: InjectFlags.Optional): T|null;
declare function inject<T>(token: Type<T>, flags: InjectFlags.Default | InjectFlags.Host | InjectFlags.Self | InjectFlags.SkipSelf): T;
declare function inject<T>(token: Type<T>, flags: InjectFlags): T|null;
```

[TypeScript playground](https://www.typescriptlang.org/play?ts=4.7.2#code/KYOwrgtgBAkiBWwDGAXAYgGwIYHMDOUA3gFBRQAiwAZlmBilALxQAMANKVABID2eDzAIwcyAZWAYqTKACYRUUQGsAlgAdxk6QBZ5AeVUplPEFgzSAHBwC+xYsAAeqngCcGykCmDOaSYFAAqAJ6qwAA8--Z3BROxUR2MJ3EWbFjq6QA)

What is concerning here though is that TypeScript always selects the _second_ overload (the one with `InjectFlags.Optional`) for inline bit masks, as can be seen in the `skipSelfAndSelf` case in the playground. This is unlike using a variable of type `InjectFlags`, which does resolve to the _last_ overload as desired.

Currently this sort of works out, especially in practice as it doesn't make sense to combine any of the `Self`, `SkipSelf` and `Host` flags into a single call. It does concern me though that TypeScript doesn't select the last overload for `skipSelfAndSelf`, as it indicates that TypeScript's overload resolution isn't equipped for handling this properly at the moment. It also means that the specific ordering of overload 2 and 3 is important, which ideally it should not be (the signatures don't have overlap in their `flags` parameter, so their relative ordering shouldn't affect overload resolution but it does).

Are these the right combinations?

```typescript
type InjectOptions = ({} | {self: true} | {skipSelf: true} | {host: true, skipSelf?: true})
type Optional = {optional: true}

function inject<T>(token: Type<T>): T;
function inject<T>(token: Type<T>, options: InjectOptions & Optional): T | null;
function inject<T>(token: Type<T>, options: InjectOptions): T;
function inject<T>(token: Type<T>, flags: InjectFlags): T | null;
function inject<T>(token: Type<T>, flagsOrOptions?: unknown) {
}

class X {}

const a: X  = inject(X);
const b: X  = inject(X, {self: true});
const c: X  = inject(X, {skipSelf: true});
const d: X  = inject(X, {host: true});
const e: X  = inject(X, {host: true, skipSelf: true});
const f: X | null  = inject(X, {optional: true});
const g: X | null  = inject(X, {self: true, optional: true});
const h: X | null  = inject(X, {skipSelf: true, optional: true});
const i: X | null  = inject(X, {host: true, optional: true});
const j: X | null  = inject(X, {host: true, skipSelf: true, optional: true});

```


